welcome back so we've been talking about  data science and machine learning and  you can't talk about machine learning  nowadays without talking about neural  networks so I want to give you a little  bit of an overview of what neural  networks are what they can do what they  can't do  strengths and limitations a bit about  their architecture how to program them  how to understand kind of where they  came from and what what you can do with  them now okay so neural networks are an  incredibly powerful and important aspect  of machine learning there are great  modeling capabilities of networks but I  do want to emphasize that neural  networks are not all that there is to  machine learning there's a lot more but  this is kind of one of the most  important and well used architectures  and machine learning today and part of  the reason we like them so much and the  community is adopting these so rapidly  is because they are very very expressive  architectures so so somehow a neural  network is a framework for learning  models from data and that framework has  a lot of degrees of freedom it's not  terribly constrained and so there's a  lot of expressive capability in these  neural networks they're able to capture  lots of phenomenon that we observe in in  the real world from example data sets  and it may be a more mathematical way of  describing what we mean by expressive is  that neural networks are good at  approximating arbitrary input/output  functions so if I have data with inputs  and outputs and I have enough training  data a rich enough and large enough  training data set then neural networks  are very very good at arbitrarily  approximating even very nasty  complicated input/output functions and  there's mathematical theory around this  that proves that if you have enough and  rich enough data and a sufficiently  large neural network architecture then  you can you can describe these very  complex functions now a huge caveat  something really important to mention is  that these neural networks work in the  big data limit when you have lots and  lots of  examples of your data that the data and  what the outcomes are so think of  Google's image library or Facebook's  image library they have so much training  data that most of what they you will see  in the future is somehow in that  training data and so neural networks are  great when you have vast training  datasets and when the thing you want to  use that neural network for in the  future is likely to be similar to what  you've seen in the past so I would call  that kind of an interpolation problem  neural networks are great for big  interpolation problems they can  approximate big nasty interpolators if  you have rich enough training data for  the for the past ok so that's important  neural networks have been around for a  really long time they've been around for  decades Rosenblatt came up with this  perceptron in 1958 this was well I think  the earliest neural network and this was  used for binary classification pretty  interesting idea and there was a  tremendous amount of interest and  investment and development in neural  networks for decades after this and  there's this idea that these neural  networks are mimicking some of the  functionality in biological systems and  animals you know humans and things like  that and so there was a lot of interest  both in the neuroscience community and  in the computer science and applied math  and statistics communities in what were  the capabilities of these but because of  limitations in the data and the  computational power at the time this  kind of stalled out in the the late 70s  and 80s and what's known as the AI  winter so there's this big overhyping of  capabilities the actual architectures  couldn't deliver because of limitations  of the time and so these fell out of  fashion for for decades until 2012 with  this image net challenge where deep  neural networks trained on vast datasets  in new computational architectures these  GPUs graphics processing units this kind  of culmination of big data big label  datasets much more powerful  computers and that could facilitate the  training of deep neural network  architectures really was the reviving  point for neural networks so in 2012  people couldn't believe how how  significant the performance improvement  in neural networks had become and they  beat all of the other leading algorithms  in in this image classification  challenge and since then in a very short  period of time neural networks have  risen to the top of the performance  metrics in many many many challenges  both in speech recognition natural  language processing translation image  classification and really are kind of  setting the standard for what is  possible in lots of these these  applications where you have huge data  sets and you want to do arbitrary  function approximation okay or kind of  interpolation based on your massive  massive training data set ok and I  should again point out that these neural  networks are inspired in principle by  biological systems so if you believe  that this eagle is performing this  incredible flight maneuver and control  and interacting with its environment  with its brain and its visual system  then in principle it has a network of  neurons that are performing this  incredibly complex task now whether or  not our artificial neural networks are  our silicon neural networks have  anything to do with the actual biology I  mean that's up for for grabs and and  there's lots and lots of research kind  of at the interface of neuroscience and  computer science to see what kind of  connections and comparisons we can make  what can we learn from biological  systems that will help our neural  networks what can we learn when we build  and design these neural networks that  can teach us about these biological  systems and a lot of this is actually  inspired by by visual systems in  particular these kind of multi-layered  information processing architectures  where you take very high resolution  information and pass it through layers  of abstraction and you know humans and  animals can pull out objects in motion  and  abstract that this collection of pixels  is an eagle or a word and we have these  abstractions okay and that's the kind of  thing that we want our neural network to  be able to do is take this very high  dimensional input data complex data with  lots of going on and distill out through  maybe these layers and processing  different abstractions that we can use  to build models okay  so I'll talk a lot more about what  neural networks are how they're built  the history and what is capable what our  capabilities are now and where it's  going okay thank you
 

